<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Georgios I of Makuria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Georgios I was king of Makuria, the medieval Christian kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, in the 9th century. In the 830s, as heir to the throne, he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and negotiated with the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutasim">al-Mu'tasim</a> over the arrears of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baqt">Baqt</a> tribute, which were cancelled. He later reigned for several decades from about 856, a period of peace and prosperity in Makuria.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Georgios, called Firaki in Arabic sources, was the son of King Zacharias III of Makuria. What is known of him comes from the Egyptian historians <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-maqrizi">al-Maqrizi</a>, al-Balawi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-taghribirdi">Ibn Taghribirdi</a>, whose accounts are not always conclusive and contradict each other at several points, so that modern historians find his life hard to reconstruct.</p><p>He first appears in 835, when he was about 20 years old. His father made him co-regent, possibly with the title of caesar, and sent him on a long journey to settle a dispute with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>. While <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> was torn by the Fourth Fitna, Zacharias had stopped paying the baqt, and once Abbasid control over Egypt was restored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> demanded that payments resume with all arrears. With the help of Patriarch Joseph I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> a new treaty was agreed, which also changed the terms so that the baqt was due only once every three years.</p><p>Georgios was back in Dongola early in 837 and seems to have ruled jointly with his father from then on. Another story has him taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> as a prisoner in his youth; he may have made the journey twice, or he may have been confused with someone else.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Zacharias died at some time before 856, by which year Georgios ruled alone. Unlike Roman emperors and his Byzantine contemporaries, he kept the title of caesar rather than taking that of augustus, which his father had used. During his reign the Arab adventurer Abu al-Rahman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-umari">al-Umari</a> invaded the gold-mining district near Abu Haman with a private army. Georgios sent his nephew and heir Niuty against him, but Niuty rebelled. A son sent next was defeated and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alodia">Alodia</a>. Another son, Zacharias, was beaten once by Niuty's forces, then allied with al-Umari, defeated Niuty and finally drove al-Umari back to the north.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the 13th century the Coptic priest Abu al-Makarim described a portrait in a Nubian church showing Georgios, son of Zacharias, as a man of eighty. The funerary inscription of the Makurian tetrarch Mariankouda, of June 887, mentions him as already dead. His immediate successor is unknown, but his son Zacharias IV came to the throne in 916 or 917, and Georgios's wife Mariam held the rank of queen mother during her son's reign.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Georgios I of Makuria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
